EKS Cluster Auto scaler
======================

The Cluster Autoscaler in Amazon EKS (Elastic Kubernetes Service) automatically adjusts the size of your EKS cluster based on the demands of your workloads. It helps manage the scaling of worker nodes in your cluster, adding nodes when there are pending pods that cannot be scheduled due to insufficient resources and removing nodes when they are underutilized.


How to set-up
-------------

step 1:

link: https://github.com/kubernetes/autoscaler/blob/master/cluster-autoscaler/cloudprovider/aws/README.md

--> create IAM policy with below rules

{
  "Version": "2012-10-17",
  "Statement": [
    {
      "Effect": "Allow",
      "Action": [
        "autoscaling:DescribeAutoScalingGroups",
        "autoscaling:DescribeAutoScalingInstances",
        "autoscaling:DescribeLaunchConfigurations",
        "autoscaling:DescribeScalingActivities",
        "ec2:DescribeImages",
        "ec2:DescribeInstanceTypes",
        "ec2:DescribeLaunchTemplateVersions",
        "ec2:GetInstanceTypesFromInstanceRequirements",
        "eks:DescribeNodegroup"
      ],
      "Resource": ["*"]
    },
    {
      "Effect": "Allow",
      "Action": [
        "autoscaling:SetDesiredCapacity",
        "autoscaling:TerminateInstanceInAutoScalingGroup"
      ],
      "Resource": ["*"]
    }
  ]
}


goto aws --> search iam --> policies --> create policy --> select json --> paste the above rules--> next --> 

policy name(ClusterAutoScalarPolicy)--> create



step 2:
-------

attach the policy to eks cluster IAM role.


select the EKS IAM role --> permissions --> add permissions --> Attach policies --> select ClusterAutoScalerpolicy--> add permissions 



step 3: Install the clusterAutoAscaller
-------

link: https://github.com/kubernetes/autoscaler/blob/master/cluster-autoscaler/cloudprovider/aws/README.md


kubectl apply -f examples/cluster-autoscaler-autodiscover.yaml

---
apiVersion: v1
kind: ServiceAccount
metadata:
  labels:
    k8s-addon: cluster-autoscaler.addons.k8s.io
    k8s-app: cluster-autoscaler
  name: cluster-autoscaler
  namespace: kube-system
---
apiVersion: rbac.authorization.k8s.io/v1
kind: ClusterRole
metadata:
  name: cluster-autoscaler
  labels:
    k8s-addon: cluster-autoscaler.addons.k8s.io
    k8s-app: cluster-autoscaler
rules:
  - apiGroups: [""]
    resources: ["events", "endpoints"]
    verbs: ["create", "patch"]
  - apiGroups: [""]
    resources: ["pods/eviction"]
    verbs: ["create"]
  - apiGroups: [""]
    resources: ["pods/status"]
    verbs: ["update"]
  - apiGroups: [""]
    resources: ["endpoints"]
    resourceNames: ["cluster-autoscaler"]
    verbs: ["get", "update"]
  - apiGroups: [""]
    resources: ["nodes"]
    verbs: ["watch", "list", "get", "update"]
  - apiGroups: [""]
    resources: ["namespaces", "pods", "services", "replicationcontrollers", "persistentvolumeclaims", "persistentvolumes"]
    verbs: ["watch", "list", "get"]
  - apiGroups: ["extensions"]
    resources: ["replicasets", "daemonsets"]
    verbs: ["watch", "list", "get"]
  - apiGroups: ["policy"]
    resources: ["poddisruptionbudgets"]
    verbs: ["watch", "list"]
  - apiGroups: ["apps"]
    resources: ["statefulsets", "replicasets", "daemonsets"]
    verbs: ["watch", "list", "get"]
  - apiGroups: ["storage.k8s.io"]
    resources: ["storageclasses", "csinodes", "csidrivers", "csistoragecapacities"]
    verbs: ["watch", "list", "get"]
  - apiGroups: ["batch", "extensions"]
    resources: ["jobs"]
    verbs: ["get", "list", "watch", "patch"]
  - apiGroups: ["coordination.k8s.io"]
    resources: ["leases"]
    verbs: ["create"]
  - apiGroups: ["coordination.k8s.io"]
    resources: ["leases"]
    resourceNames: ["cluster-autoscaler"]
    verbs: ["get", "update"]
---
apiVersion: rbac.authorization.k8s.io/v1
kind: Role
metadata:
  name: cluster-autoscaler
  namespace: kube-system
  labels:
    k8s-addon: cluster-autoscaler.addons.k8s.io
    k8s-app: cluster-autoscaler
rules:
  - apiGroups: [""]
    resources: ["configmaps"]
    verbs: ["create", "list", "watch"]
  - apiGroups: [""]
    resources: ["configmaps"]
    resourceNames: ["cluster-autoscaler-status", "cluster-autoscaler-priority-expander"]
    verbs: ["delete", "get", "update", "watch"]
---
apiVersion: rbac.authorization.k8s.io/v1
kind: ClusterRoleBinding
metadata:
  name: cluster-autoscaler
  labels:
    k8s-addon: cluster-autoscaler.addons.k8s.io
    k8s-app: cluster-autoscaler
roleRef:
  apiGroup: rbac.authorization.k8s.io
  kind: ClusterRole
  name: cluster-autoscaler
subjects:
  - kind: ServiceAccount
    name: cluster-autoscaler
    namespace: kube-system
---
apiVersion: rbac.authorization.k8s.io/v1
kind: RoleBinding
metadata:
  name: cluster-autoscaler
  namespace: kube-system
  labels:
    k8s-addon: cluster-autoscaler.addons.k8s.io
    k8s-app: cluster-autoscaler
roleRef:
  apiGroup: rbac.authorization.k8s.io
  kind: Role
  name: cluster-autoscaler
subjects:
  - kind: ServiceAccount
    name: cluster-autoscaler
    namespace: kube-system
---
apiVersion: apps/v1
kind: Deployment
metadata:
  name: cluster-autoscaler
  namespace: kube-system
  labels:
    app: cluster-autoscaler
spec:
  replicas: 1
  selector:
    matchLabels:
      app: cluster-autoscaler
  template:
    metadata:
      labels:
        app: cluster-autoscaler
      annotations:
        prometheus.io/scrape: "true"
        prometheus.io/port: "8085"
    spec:
      priorityClassName: system-cluster-critical
      securityContext:
        runAsNonRoot: true
        runAsUser: 65534
        fsGroup: 65534
        seccompProfile:
          type: RuntimeDefault
      serviceAccountName: cluster-autoscaler
      containers:
        - name: cluster-autoscaler
          image: registry.k8s.io/autoscaling/cluster-autoscaler:v1.26.2
          imagePullPolicy: Always
          resources:
            limits:
              cpu: 100m
              memory: 600Mi
            requests:
              cpu: 100m
              memory: 600Mi
          command:
            - ./cluster-autoscaler
            - --v=4
            - --stderrthreshold=info
            - --cloud-provider=aws
            - --skip-nodes-with-local-storage=false
            - --expander=least-waste
            - --node-group-auto-discovery=asg:tag=k8s.io/cluster-autoscaler/enabled,k8s.io/cluster-autoscaler/my-demo-cluster
          securityContext:
            allowPrivilegeEscalation: false
            readOnlyRootFilesystem: true
            capabilities:
              drop: ["ALL"]
          volumeMounts:
            - name: ssl-certs
              mountPath: /etc/ssl/certs/ca-certificates.crt
              readOnly: true
      volumes:
        - name: ssl-certs
          hostPath:
            # for Ubuntu/Debian nodes. For Amazon Linux, use /etc/ssl/certs/ca-bundle.crt
            path: /etc/ssl/certs/ca-bundle.crt





NOTE: pls change the cluster name and apply.



explaination:




Step 1 — Create IAM policy
Your policy starts with:
{
  "Version": "2012-10-17",
  "Statement": [...]
}
This is an AWS IAM policy.
It tells AWS:
"What AWS API operations is this identity allowed to perform?"

First statement — read/inspect AWS resources
You have:
"Effect": "Allow",
"Action": [
  "autoscaling:DescribeAutoScalingGroups",
  "autoscaling:DescribeAutoScalingInstances",
  "autoscaling:DescribeLaunchConfigurations",
  "autoscaling:DescribeScalingActivities",
  "ec2:DescribeImages",
  "ec2:DescribeInstanceTypes",
  "ec2:DescribeLaunchTemplateVersions",
  "ec2:GetInstanceTypesFromInstanceRequirements",
  "eks:DescribeNodegroup"
],
"Resource": ["*"]
Think of these as:
"Cluster Autoscaler is allowed to LOOK at AWS information."

For example:
autoscaling:DescribeAutoScalingGroups
Cluster Autoscaler needs to know:
What Auto Scaling Groups exist?
What is their desired size?
What is their minimum?
What is their maximum?
autoscaling:DescribeAutoScalingInstances
It can ask:
Which EC2 instances belong to which Auto Scaling Group?

autoscaling:DescribeScalingActivities
It can check:
What scaling activities have happened?

For example:
ASG increased:
2 → 3 instances
ec2:DescribeImages
It can inspect EC2 AMIs.
ec2:DescribeInstanceTypes
It can find information about instance types such as:
t3.medium
t3.large
m5.large
including their resources.
ec2:DescribeLaunchTemplateVersions
It can inspect launch template versions used by node groups.
eks:DescribeNodegroup
It can inspect EKS node group information.
Why are these called Describe?
Because they are primarily read/inspection operations.
Think:
Describe = LOOK
The first statement essentially gives Cluster Autoscaler permission to:
LOOK at AWS resources
4. Second statement — actually change capacity
Now look at:
"Effect": "Allow",
"Action": [
  "autoscaling:SetDesiredCapacity",
  "autoscaling:TerminateInstanceInAutoScalingGroup"
],
"Resource": ["*"]
This is different.
These permissions allow Cluster Autoscaler to change the Auto Scaling Group.
SetDesiredCapacity
Suppose:
ASG desired capacity = 2
Cluster Autoscaler decides another node is required.
It can effectively request:
desired capacity:
2 → 3
AWS Auto Scaling then launches another EC2 instance.
TerminateInstanceInAutoScalingGroup
This is used when Cluster Autoscaler needs to scale down.
For example:
Node1
Node2
Node3
If Node3 is no longer needed, Cluster Autoscaler can initiate removal of an instance from the Auto Scaling Group.
So:
Scale UP
2 nodes → 3 nodes
        ↑
SetDesiredCapacity

Scale DOWN
3 nodes → 2 nodes
        ↑
TerminateInstanceInAutoScalingGroup
5. Why does Cluster Autoscaler need IAM?
Because Cluster Autoscaler is running inside Kubernetes, but it needs to control AWS resources.
There are two different permission systems involved:
Kubernetes permissions
        +
AWS permissions
Kubernetes RBAC
Controls:
What can Cluster Autoscaler do inside Kubernetes?

AWS IAM
Controls:
What can Cluster Autoscaler do in AWS?

This is why your setup contains both:
Kubernetes RBAC YAML
and:
AWS IAM Policy
Step 2 — Attach IAM policy
Your instructions say:
Attach the policy to the EKS cluster IAM role.

Conceptually:
IAM Policy
ClusterAutoScalerPolicy
        │
        ↓
EKS IAM Role
        │
        ↓
AWS permissions
The policy gives permissions such as:
Describe ASGs
Describe EC2
Set desired capacity
Terminate ASG instance
But there is an important modern-EKS point here
The Cluster Autoscaler Pod needs AWS permissions. AWS now supports IRSA and EKS Pod Identity specifically for giving Kubernetes workloads fine-grained IAM permissions. AWS documents these as mechanisms for granting IAM permissions to workloads, and recommends scoping roles to the specific service account when using IRSA. AWS Documentation
So I would not blindly follow the old instruction of attaching this policy to the EKS cluster role for a new production setup.
For learning, however, the important conceptual point is:
Cluster Autoscaler
       ↓
needs AWS API access
       ↓
IAM permissions
       ↓
AWS Auto Scaling
Step 3 — Install Cluster Autoscaler
Now we move from AWS to Kubernetes.
You run:
kubectl apply -f examples/cluster-autoscaler-autodiscover.yaml
This YAML creates several Kubernetes objects.
Let's go through them one by one.
6. ServiceAccount
kind: ServiceAccount
metadata:
  name: cluster-autoscaler
  namespace: kube-system
A ServiceAccount is an identity for a Pod inside Kubernetes.
Think:
Human user
    ↓
IAM user/role

Pod
    ↓
Kubernetes ServiceAccount
Your Cluster Autoscaler Pod will use:
ServiceAccount:
cluster-autoscaler
7. ClusterRole
Next:
kind: ClusterRole
metadata:
  name: cluster-autoscaler
This defines:
What Kubernetes resources can Cluster Autoscaler access and what operations can it perform?

For example:
resources: ["nodes"]
verbs: ["watch", "list", "get", "update"]
It can:
get
list
watch
update
Nodes.
8. What are resources and verbs?
This is important.
Suppose you see:
resources:
- nodes

verbs:
- get
- list
- watch
It means:
Cluster Autoscaler can GET, LIST and WATCH Kubernetes Nodes.

Resource
nodes
pods
services
deployments
persistentvolumeclaims
Verb
get
list
watch
create
update
patch
delete
Think:
RESOURCE = WHAT?

VERB = WHAT CAN I DO?
9. Why does Cluster Autoscaler need Pods?
You have:
resources:
- namespaces
- pods
- services
- replicationcontrollers
- persistentvolumeclaims
- persistentvolumes

verbs:
- watch
- list
- get
Why?
Because Cluster Autoscaler needs to understand the state of the cluster.
For example:
Are Pods pending?

Why are they pending?

Can they fit on existing nodes?

Would another node help?
It needs information about Pods, nodes, workloads, storage, etc.
10. Why does it need pods/eviction?
You have:
resources:
- pods/eviction
verbs:
- create
This is related to safely evicting Pods when scaling down a node.
For example:
Node3
 ├── Pod A
 ├── Pod B
 └── Pod C
If Cluster Autoscaler wants to remove Node3, it cannot simply disappear without considering the Pods.
It can use Kubernetes eviction mechanisms so workloads can be rescheduled appropriately.
11. ClusterRoleBinding
Now:
kind: ClusterRoleBinding
This is the connection between:
ClusterRole
and:
ServiceAccount
You have:
roleRef:
  kind: ClusterRole
  name: cluster-autoscaler

subjects:
- kind: ServiceAccount
  name: cluster-autoscaler
  namespace: kube-system
Meaning:
ClusterRole
cluster-autoscaler
       │
       │ gives permissions to
       ↓
ServiceAccount
cluster-autoscaler
       │
       ↓
Cluster Autoscaler Pod
Without the binding, simply creating the ClusterRole doesn't give the ServiceAccount those permissions.
12. Role and RoleBinding
You also have:
kind: Role
namespace: kube-system
and:
kind: RoleBinding
namespace: kube-system
Why both ClusterRole and Role?
Because Kubernetes has two scopes.
ClusterRole
Cluster-wide permissions.
For example:
Nodes
Pods
Namespaces
PV
StorageClass
Role
Permissions within a particular namespace.
Here:
kube-system
The Role allows access to ConfigMaps:
resources:
- configmaps
So:
ClusterRole
    ↓
cluster-wide resources

Role
    ↓
kube-system namespace resources
13. Finally, the Deployment
This is the most important part.
kind: Deployment
metadata:
  name: cluster-autoscaler
  namespace: kube-system
This actually creates the Cluster Autoscaler Pod.
You have:
replicas: 1
So:
Deployment
     ↓
1 Cluster Autoscaler Pod
14. Which image is being used?
image: registry.k8s.io/autoscaling/cluster-autoscaler:v1.26.2
This is the Cluster Autoscaler application.
When the Pod starts, this executable runs:
./cluster-autoscaler
Again, v1.26.2 is from the old example you pasted; use a version compatible with your EKS/Kubernetes version rather than copying it blindly. The current upstream example uses a newer image. GitHub
15. --cloud-provider=aws
- --cloud-provider=aws
This tells Cluster Autoscaler:
"I am running against AWS."

Therefore it knows that it should interact with:
AWS Auto Scaling
EC2
EKS
16. The most important line: auto-discovery
This line:
- --node-group-auto-discovery=asg:tag=k8s.io/cluster-autoscaler/enabled,k8s.io/cluster-autoscaler/my-demo-cluster
is extremely important.
It tells Cluster Autoscaler:
Find the AWS Auto Scaling Groups that have these tags.

The two tags are:
k8s.io/cluster-autoscaler/enabled
and:
k8s.io/cluster-autoscaler/my-demo-cluster
AWS's Cluster Autoscaler documentation describes this auto-discovery approach: the ASGs must have the required tags so the autoscaler can discover the node groups. GitHub
17. What does this mean in practice?
Suppose your EKS cluster is:
my-demo-cluster
and your worker node ASG has:
Tag:
k8s.io/cluster-autoscaler/enabled = true

Tag:
k8s.io/cluster-autoscaler/my-demo-cluster = owned
Then:
Cluster Autoscaler
       │
       │ looks for these tags
       ↓
AWS Auto Scaling Groups
       │
       ├── ASG-1 ✅
       ├── ASG-2 ❌
       └── ASG-3 ❌
It discovers ASG-1 as a node group that it can manage.
eksctl can add these tags when creating node groups with autoscaling access enabled. AWS Documentation
18. --expander=least-waste
- --expander=least-waste
Suppose two node groups could potentially accommodate pending Pods:
Node Group A → t3.medium
Node Group B → t3.large
The least-waste expander uses a strategy intended to minimize unused capacity after scaling. The exact choice can matter when multiple node groups are eligible.
For now, don't worry too much about this option.
19. Resource requests and limits
You have:
resources:
  limits:
    cpu: 100m
    memory: 600Mi
  requests:
    cpu: 100m
    memory: 600Mi
This is Kubernetes resource management.
100m CPU
means approximately:
0.1 CPU core
and:
600Mi
means approximately 600 MiB memory.
So the Cluster Autoscaler Pod requests:
CPU:    0.1 core
Memory: 600 MiB
20. Why priorityClassName?
priorityClassName: system-cluster-critical
This gives the Cluster Autoscaler Pod a high scheduling priority.
Why?
Because Cluster Autoscaler is an important component for cluster operation.
You don't want a situation where:
Cluster is full
      ↓
Cluster Autoscaler itself cannot run
      ↓
It cannot add nodes
      ↓
Cluster cannot recover easily
So it receives high priority.
21. serviceAccountName
This connects the Deployment to the ServiceAccount:
serviceAccountName: cluster-autoscaler
So:
Deployment
     ↓
Pod
     ↓
serviceAccountName:
cluster-autoscaler
     ↓
ServiceAccount
     ↓
ClusterRoleBinding
     ↓
ClusterRole permissions
This is the Kubernetes RBAC side.
For AWS access, modern EKS setups should additionally associate the ServiceAccount with an IAM role using IRSA or EKS Pod Identity rather than broadly relying on a cluster role. AWS Documentation
22. Security settings
You have:
runAsNonRoot: true
runAsUser: 65534
This means:
Don't run the application as Linux root.

65534 is commonly used for a non-root user such as nobody.
Then:
allowPrivilegeEscalation: false
means:
Don't allow the container to gain additional privileges.

And:
capabilities:
  drop:
  - ALL
means:
Drop Linux capabilities.

And:
readOnlyRootFilesystem: true
means:
The container's root filesystem should be read-only.

These are security-hardening measures.
23. Why mount SSL certificates?
At the bottom:
volumes:
- name: ssl-certs
  hostPath:
    path: /etc/ssl/certs/ca-bundle.crt
And:
volumeMounts:
- name: ssl-certs
  mountPath: /etc/ssl/certs/ca-certificates.crt
  readOnly: true
Cluster Autoscaler needs to communicate securely with AWS APIs.
HTTPS connections require trusted CA certificates.
So the container gets the node's CA certificate bundle.
Think:
EC2 node
   │
   │ CA certificates
   ↓
Cluster Autoscaler container
   │
   │ HTTPS
   ↓
AWS APIs
24. Put everything together
Now the entire setup becomes much easier:
                    EKS CLUSTER
                         │
                         │
               ┌─────────▼─────────┐
               │ Cluster Autoscaler│
               │       Pod         │
               └─────────┬─────────┘
                         │
              Kubernetes │ API
                         │
              ┌──────────▼──────────┐
              │ Kubernetes RBAC     │
              │                     │
              │ ServiceAccount      │
              │       ↓             │
              │ ClusterRole         │
              │       ↓             │
              │ ClusterRoleBinding  │
              └─────────────────────┘
                         │
                         │ AWS API
                         │
              ┌──────────▼──────────┐
              │       AWS IAM       │
              │                     │
              │ Describe resources  │
              │ SetDesiredCapacity  │
              │ Terminate instance  │
              └──────────┬──────────┘
                         │
                         ▼
                AWS Auto Scaling Group
                         │
                ┌────────┴────────┐
                │                 │
              EC2 Node          EC2 Node
                │                 │
                └────────┬────────┘
                         │
                    Kubernetes Pods
25. The actual scale-up process
This is the most important flow to understand.
Imagine:
Node1     Node2
  │         │
  └────┬────┘
       │
    Pods running
Now you deploy more Pods:
Pod1 Pod2 Pod3 Pod4 Pod5
Suppose there isn't enough CPU.
Scheduler says:
Pod5
  ↓
Pending
  ↓
Insufficient CPU
Cluster Autoscaler notices:
Pending Pod
     ↓
Existing nodes cannot accommodate it
Then:
Cluster Autoscaler
        ↓
Find matching ASG
        ↓
AWS Auto Scaling API
        ↓
Desired capacity:
2 → 3
        ↓
AWS launches EC2
        ↓
EC2 joins EKS
        ↓
New Kubernetes Node
        ↓
Scheduler places Pod5
26. Scale-down process
The reverse can happen.
Node1   Node2   Node3
Suppose Node3 is mostly empty.
Cluster Autoscaler determines that workloads can run on fewer nodes.
Then:
Cluster Autoscaler
       ↓
Choose removable node
       ↓
Evict/reschedule Pods appropriately
       ↓
AWS Auto Scaling
       ↓
Terminate instance
Result:
Node1   Node2
27. One thing missing from your instructions
For auto-discovery to work, your worker-node Auto Scaling Group must have the required tags.
For cluster name my-demo-cluster, typically:
k8s.io/cluster-autoscaler/enabled = true
k8s.io/cluster-autoscaler/my-demo-cluster = owned
The exact values can vary; the current Cluster Autoscaler documentation says the required tag keys are what discovery looks for unless values are explicitly specified. GitHub
So your setup is really:
1. IAM
   ↓
AWS permission

2. Kubernetes RBAC
   ↓
Kubernetes permission

3. ServiceAccount / AWS Pod identity
   ↓
Connect workload identity to AWS permissions

4. Cluster Autoscaler Deployment
   ↓
Run autoscaler program

5. ASG tags
   ↓
Tell autoscaler which node groups to manage

6. Pending Pods
   ↓
Autoscaler increases node count
The three most important things to remember
IAM policy → What can Cluster Autoscaler do in AWS?
ClusterRole/RBAC → What can Cluster Autoscaler do inside Kubernetes?
Deployment → Actually runs the Cluster Autoscaler program.
And the most important relationship is:
Cluster Autoscaler
       │
       ├── Kubernetes API
       │       ↓
       │    understand Pods/Nodes
       │
       └── AWS API
               ↓
          change ASG size
               ↓
          add/remove EC2 nodes
One final caution: don't use the pasted v1.26.2 image just because it appears in a tutorial. Match the Cluster Autoscaler version to your Kubernetes/EKS version and use the current upstream/AWS instructions. The upstream example has moved on to newer versions. GitHub





    



















